  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1073,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1089,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152082,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152083,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152084,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most arise in the paratubal/para-ovarian soft tissues {33534223; 39169716}. Rare examples originate in the ovary, fallopian tube, uterine serosa, or abdominopelvic peritoneum.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152085,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients usually present with symptoms related to a pelvic mass; 10&ndash;15% are incidental {33534223; 39169716}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152086,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are rare neoplasms. Patients present over a wide age range (16&ndash;74&nbsp;years; median: 40&nbsp;years) {33534223; 39169716}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152087,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately 30% occur in association with Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome {33534223; 38919071; 39169716; 39801168}. For more comprehensive information on Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome, refer to <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/290\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome (STK11).</em></p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152088,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cell of origin of <em>STK11</em> adnexal tumour is unknown. These tumours are defined by <em>STK11</em> loss-of-function genomic alterations, including point mutations, indels, structural rearrangements, and deletions {33534223; 39169716}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152089,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range from 40 to 225&nbsp;mm, with a median size of 110&nbsp;mm {33534223; 39169716}. They are usually solid or solid and cystic, with frequent haemorrhage and necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152090,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>STK11</em> adnexal tumours exhibit a spectrum of histological patterns, with the most common being interanastomosing cords and trabeculae {33534223; 39169716}. Other patterns include cribriform, tubular, cystic, acinar, papillary, nested, and solid. The stroma is often myxoid, but it may also be oedematous, hyalinized, or loosely collagenous. Cells are usually columnar to cuboidal and uniformly atypical with round to ovoid nuclei, vesicular chromatin, prominent nucleoli, and eosinophilic to clear cytoplasm; overt pleomorphism is absent. Spindled cells can be observed but are rarely dominant. Signet ring&ndash;like cells or whorling of tumour cells is occasionally seen. Mitoses are variable and necrosis is uncommon.</p>\r\n<p>STK11 shows complete loss of cytoplasmic staining {39169716}. The immunohistochemical profile is otherwise nonspecific, with variable expression of keratins, sex cord markers, mesothelial markers, hormone receptors, CD10, and desmin {33534223; 38919071; 39169716}. PAX8, EMA, GATA3, and SF1 may show limited expression. Claudin-4, FOXL2, and TTF1 are negative. p53 is wild type.</p>\r\n<p>Although <em>STK11</em> adnexal tumour and Wolffian tumour may exhibit overlapping morphological and immunohistochemical features, they should be distinguished from each other due to their contrasting prognosis and hereditary syndrome risk.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152091,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152092,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular testing with an assay covering the entire coding region of <em>STK11</em> and assessing for copy-number alterations is helpful for the diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152093,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> multiple histological patterns including interanastomosing cords and trabeculae; myxoid, oedematous, or hyalinized stroma; STK11 immunohistochemical loss and/or <em>STK11</em> genomic alterations if not Peutz&ndash;Jeghers related.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152094,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A staging system has not been established.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152095,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 90,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours are aggressive, with metastatic disease at diagnosis in 50&ndash;60% and recurrences in 80% {33534223; 39169716}. Disease-related death occurs in approximately 50%, but follow-up is limited {40262437}. Prognostic features have not been identified.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:55:18"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]